      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-b/q5-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"In the original sign convention, differentiation gives (6y + 11x + 2)y′ + 11y −22x −8 = 0. [M1] [A1] Therefore y′ = 22x −11y + 8 6y + 11x + 2 , k = 11 . [M1] [A1] (b) With x = 2, the equation with positive 11x2 reads 44 −3y2 −22y + 16 −2y = 0. Hence the simplified curve equation gives 3y2 + 24y −60 = 0, [A1] or y2 + 8y −20 = 0 on division by 3. Factorising gives (y −2)(y + 10) = 0. [M1] Both values satisfy the original denominator restriction: [A1] The gradients at (2, 2) and (2, −10) are (44 −22 + 8)/(12 + 22 + 2) = 30/36 [A1] = 5/6 and (44 + 110 + 8)/(−60 + 22 + 2) = 162/(−36) , [A1] = −9/2\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-b/q5-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"respectively: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:148.0px\"></figure>",
